#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82a959f-469c-580d-b699-744a01e1a73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f82a959f-469c-580d-b699-744a01e1a73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234938} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d3bdac-6753-510d-b7d4-b1bb408d2f9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a37e3e9-7f1b-5a8d-b564-d8b93eaac32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167324" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235739 :step/id #uuid "c2c98eb5-4423-57c5-b4bb-0778bf19de27"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0803874-a90d-56fd-a574-502ea3d987fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0803874-a90d-56fd-a574-502ea3d987fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234925} :answer-field/id #uuid "a145d34e-2f2d-57fd-861d-ae23542378fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf5f6035-a258-5a14-8838-a24ee5796c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167325" :question/problem "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235608 :step/id #uuid "2333340e-3c9a-5b16-8993-0d2fe8215e8e"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918} :answer-field/id #uuid "3429cda5-daf9-53dc-9c18-72e4afbade0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dabe687a-249f-595c-be70-5214c92992e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167326" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235760 :step/id #uuid "df370e4a-e63e-5c70-ac9a-a978dbca00d2"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9641fb-c8f3-5eae-84db-0c0deb61bedb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dde111b-f4d3-5f08-91c6-efd9593de57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167327" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$\\,y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235646 :step/id #uuid "4abcd3e4-0f51-5cb0-93cd-5640fb98ea06"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da92529-d318-5dfa-bfa7-0df43fae7490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c7fd239-c698-5700-aea8-d260b2cef38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167328" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235781 :step/id #uuid "f617ffe7-6161-59bb-b77e-0e7a1dc8bcfe"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340d32ab-0292-55d6-ad2a-da14464f67e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340d32ab-0292-55d6-ad2a-da14464f67e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6445949-abe5-548c-ab63-85eaeea6ef0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2e5f110-a6b6-523d-8a9c-2c0d4e144aec" :question/math-academy-id "q-167329" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=${{field-1}}${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235765 :step/id #uuid "e30fef8f-422e-5864-bfeb-bdac6f8ee291"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f74b2bd2-a95c-5b62-907c-ae6cda7b7089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f74b2bd2-a95c-5b62-907c-ae6cda7b7089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234936} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8b47e0-d44a-50a7-b006-cdcb0702528f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19dc50e5-0f97-5ac3-ba2b-ebf4a9bbf79f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167331" :question/problem "$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235626 :step/id #uuid "2fa7aeca-df1d-5e67-a24b-fbde1f9b6680"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4efd78ec-0961-543d-89f6-569515b073d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4efd78ec-0961-543d-89f6-569515b073d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234669} :answer-field/id #uuid "a091f6bc-b989-5a26-a703-2d9db59566b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d250de2a-60d6-5a40-8247-2b544c01b955" :question/math-academy-id "q-167332" :question/problem "$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235726 :step/id #uuid "afefc61a-58f9-524e-b635-c3e26fa45569"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff132e5-2ee2-59aa-8aea-3502c866b4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff132e5-2ee2-59aa-8aea-3502c866b4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234842} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4cf055c-8ce0-5492-8beb-32c82e4064bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cb1f8a0-05ce-5136-9c47-0498f7e83853" :question/math-academy-id "q-167778" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235771 :step/id #uuid "e8b0cecf-fa56-5beb-960d-5df3c4ba8c53"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3cb5e0-0aab-5830-9d6d-c2f1e718af9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a3cb5e0-0aab-5830-9d6d-c2f1e718af9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234757} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e373de0-a688-5868-b03b-0611dc9458f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "321a6511-60f4-5506-a94d-91240e0772aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-167779" :question/problem "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235650 :step/id #uuid "4d60a6ed-03c8-5d0f-a66c-8cb1c728d8df"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fb57fa-80a8-5893-88ed-795c73c49674" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8af2c303-b3aa-5790-885e-7a978a609bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167780" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235635 :step/id #uuid "3d4ac222-299d-5bd7-9a81-f6de56ddab69"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a661154-3b70-50d8-a6af-d295b9a56bb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d970612-b8f0-51a2-9d52-e3fa3b8f917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167781" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$\\,y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235702 :step/id #uuid "98a08d84-00b1-534d-bffa-e91d5d6c0249"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbff55-8f3a-5c54-81ca-c8b7d5227587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ea7df7-6856-52ff-9d9c-29beb9f68c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167782" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})=\\frac{e^x}{8\\sin^2(x)}\\bigl(${{field-1}}$\\bigr)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235688 :step/id #uuid "808c3d9b-90fc-5c3f-a556-41491f528abf"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d81f09a-0feb-5148-a473-414d681185ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d81f09a-0feb-5148-a473-414d681185ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c18185-4f4a-5388-bff5-21e01260a961" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e678b8-dc07-54a9-88b9-306f52add57d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167783" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235603 :step/id #uuid "211e6c15-d8fe-57b2-8997-87e935656c05"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98bc1b2f-fa8a-5961-97fa-ddd45efbed08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98bc1b2f-fa8a-5961-97fa-ddd45efbed08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234781} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07bf729-338c-54c6-bcd6-05c9494ec558" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6267a25-9bc8-5804-8ff9-a3389d082f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6267a25-9bc8-5804-8ff9-a3389d082f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234878} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1af1d84-8c6b-5668-ad6d-a9c0ec2f60b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e8d54b3-157f-5822-ab7d-9b5417e23e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-167784" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=${{field-1}} and $x\\,=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/36/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235772 :step/id #uuid "e9e746c7-e75b-508c-876b-987b9813c280"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e925cb33-a34c-536e-9ec6-fc4ac693e32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e925cb33-a34c-536e-9ec6-fc4ac693e32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234915} :answer-field/id #uuid "747ebec0-6972-5890-bd56-72dc24b81b4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6da96d34-46d1-5bca-95e5-7bacca66a935" :question/math-academy-id "q-167785" :question/problem "$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235701 :step/id #uuid "988c8690-6ee3-52c6-bac7-0dfb284825d3"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf72517-3161-51c9-a14c-e5763bd0a6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf72517-3161-51c9-a14c-e5763bd0a6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234788} :answer-field/id #uuid "d167882f-97cc-578a-888d-a466d8df8618" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f4bfc51-e9ec-5f3d-bb23-fc7c6d021c8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167786" :question/problem "$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235600 :step/id #uuid "1e636c0d-7ca1-5bf6-923e-cbf32ef0ca14"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ecd7d48-e192-56ba-a9eb-6c4280135677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ecd7d48-e192-56ba-a9eb-6c4280135677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234667} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1bfa42a-8d4e-593c-aa9b-c50e784fddb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "615b21ea-17f4-5ce5-8433-316c3df76ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167787" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)\n$$\n\nThe order of this differential equation is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We have the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235787 :step/id #uuid "f91e6c96-fd77-52a7-8123-4740e872ed20"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a232ba6-fdfc-512b-a78c-01e0aa14d08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a232ba6-fdfc-512b-a78c-01e0aa14d08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513601} :answer-field/id #uuid "685c059e-7098-5c75-b848-36830bd2c7c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2932e26e-2b57-549a-866d-7abe171c7f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-166078" :question/problem "$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514544 :step/id #uuid "f27a5cb2-e0b1-5191-9fed-3d27d4175a6b"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73650fb-8ca5-5747-bbe3-f451e92a6a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-4\\sin x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73650fb-8ca5-5747-bbe3-f451e92a6a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-4\\sin x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514054} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb0216fd-fe08-5818-afd9-7fc21cfab6bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d877a64-29c8-5e32-8920-d62abb73e697" :question/math-academy-id "q-166076" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514511 :step/id #uuid "770fb640-115f-5370-812e-c9a5f929df77"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75bfff0a-4c65-5fd2-b1ef-35a173aa2b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75bfff0a-4c65-5fd2-b1ef-35a173aa2b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513842} :answer-field/id #uuid "826eda45-40bd-506b-a5bb-0ddbb36d06dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60d8ee2b-aed9-5a87-bc1d-99d095c196bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-166072" :question/problem "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514502 :step/id #uuid "52c4219e-a8d2-540b-930a-53ae79e3d7aa"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f4e9c3e-c075-5ee8-b58c-ef68c609d2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)}{8\\sin^2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f4e9c3e-c075-5ee8-b58c-ef68c609d2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)}{8\\sin^2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513862} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba412a72-8abe-5840-811a-70be04c017e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "709bef3c-1deb-5177-94a0-6a0cd6bca192" :question/math-academy-id "q-166075" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514501 :step/id #uuid "50fe811b-de32-57c9-9c1e-57847199bb20"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569b842d-17a2-53d4-9b00-9b5178c0a749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569b842d-17a2-53d4-9b00-9b5178c0a749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513767} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a994c66-aac8-5b19-bc23-3cf37a49b2cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7323da16-6dcc-5329-81f1-5b6cb91bd332" :question/math-academy-id "q-166079" :question/problem "$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514508 :step/id #uuid "69e93e1d-5d1a-5360-9dcc-40c766d917f2"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597} :answer-field/id #uuid "059506ee-d597-56af-bfce-bf17819d6958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "745a29fd-db69-5896-9d5f-b9b3dcaa95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-166074" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 4, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514506 :step/id #uuid "6318fb84-c369-5d02-a31f-f2cf6cab22b3"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f06a75e2-cb48-5e18-9b9c-3baddcbbb99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f06a75e2-cb48-5e18-9b9c-3baddcbbb99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514159} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c175b32-cf9f-563c-b23b-dd0064f3ca32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83dc5138-e470-5b8f-b97c-b3006ebeb428" :question/math-academy-id "q-166071" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514509 :step/id #uuid "710f4f2b-0d09-5252-85a4-0e43efb200b1"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f634bf-1fab-5c5c-9faf-cca696e95edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f634bf-1fab-5c5c-9faf-cca696e95edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514126} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49d4659-1c96-5a65-a285-84ed00e2cd31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1eaed8d-b238-536c-b3a6-a6e7f9e94a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-166073" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514493 :step/id #uuid "20a1a95d-5d23-50cc-a5eb-c111e96d8376"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233b9c64-ca46-556b-a55b-afac855b6ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233b9c64-ca46-556b-a55b-afac855b6ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513630} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1f1a16-dad9-5c77-b873-2409266dc186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578b231b-5611-5088-bf50-85af5441c79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578b231b-5611-5088-bf50-85af5441c79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e547d708-bc0b-5d82-8783-16718788d1d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d78b3b66-caba-5b75-9179-7631fdc0e796" :question/math-academy-id "q-166077" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ when $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n." :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/36/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514498 :step/id #uuid "49ad54e7-0681-59d7-bdbd-37e117d97c3e"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c3822e0-7f7b-5338-85bf-9c5070788c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c3822e0-7f7b-5338-85bf-9c5070788c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513825} :answer-field/id #uuid "43939460-e1b0-596e-a7b7-ed6180d6c370" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbda5c07-3c3f-5393-8bd3-32f9b514a1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-166070" :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ g(x), & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind the value of the constant $k$ that makes $p(x)$ continuous at $x=3.$\n\n$k\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514491 :step/id #uuid "1cdb8aa6-8ecf-556b-897d-ed5387e45285"}]}]}
